{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויצא יעקב", :translit "vayetza ya'akov", :gloss "and Jacob went out"} {:he "יצא בסתר וברח בהחבא", :translit "yatza ba'seter u'varach ba'chaba", :gloss "he went out secretly and fled in hiding"} {:he "לו לאשה", :translit "lo le'ishah", :gloss "to him for a wife"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Parashah setumah observation: eleven instances in the Torah"}], :frame {:text "The Ba'al HaTurim notes that this pericope is a setumah (closed paragraph break) and that there are eleven such closed pericopes across the Torah's sedarim. He links the closed format to Jacob's departure in secret and flight in hiding (Genesis 28:10, where Jacob flees from Esau). He also notes the juxtaposition: the preceding verse mentions a wife (Genesis 28:9, Mahalath), and immediately afterward it says 'And Jacob went out,' indicating he went out to take a wife.", :sources ["Genesis 28:9" "Genesis 28:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויצא יעקב מבאר", :translit "vayetza ya'akov mi'be'er", :gloss "and Jacob went out from Be'er"} {:he "פנה זיוה הודה והדרה", :translit "panah zivo hodo vihadaro", :gloss "His splendor departed, His majesty and His beauty"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ויצא יעקב מבאר", :b "פנה זיוה הודה והדרה"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'His splendor departed, His majesty and His beauty' echoes the prophetic language of Ezekiel's chariot vision, where the prophet describes the divine presence in terms of hod and hadar. The gematria equates the departure of Jacob from Be'er with the departure of divine radiance, suggesting that when Jacob left, the divine splendor accompanying him departed from that place.", :sources ["Ezekiel 1:28" "Ezekiel 3:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "יעקב מבאר שבע", :translit "ya'akov mi'be'er sheva", :gloss "Jacob from Be'er Sheba"} {:he "עבר שנטמן בבית עבר", :translit "ever she'nitman be'beit ever", :gloss "Ever, who was hidden in Beth Eber"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "יעקב מבאר שבע", :spells "עבר"}], :frame {:text "The sofei tevot (final letters) of 'יעקב מבאר שבע' spell עבר (Ever). The reference to Ever being hidden in Beth Eber recalls the tradition that Shem and Ever maintained houses of study. Jacob is described as having studied in the academy of Ever before departing to Laban's house.", :sources ["Genesis 25:27" "Rashi on Genesis 25:27 citing Megillah 17a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויצא יעקב מבאר שבע", :translit "vayetza ya'akov mi'be'er sheva", :gloss "and Jacob went out from Be'er Sheba"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ויצא יעקב מבאר שבע", :spells "ארבע"}], :frame {:text "The sofei tevot (final letters) of the phrase 'ויצא יעקב מבאר שבע' spell ארבע (four). The Ba'al HaTurim does not elaborate on the significance here, leaving the symbolic resonance of the number four unstated.", :sources ["Genesis 28:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מבאר שבע", :translit "mi'be'er sheva", :gloss "from Be'er Sheba"} {:he "ער", :translit "ar", :gloss "Ar"} {:he "שי״ן של שבע, עשר", :translit "shel sheva, eser", :gloss "the shin of Sheva, ten"} {:he "שי״ד שנים נטמן בבית עבר", :translit "shneim nitman be'beit ever", :gloss "two, hidden in Beth Eber"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "מבאר שבע", :spells "ער"} {:kind :other, :note "Letter play: shin of Sheva counts as ten (via at-bash or similar); combined with ער yields additional encoded readings"}], :frame {:text "The sofei tevot of 'מבאר שבע' spell ער (Ar). The Ba'al HaTurim then combines this with additional letter-number calculations involving the shin of 'שבע' and references to Ever being hidden in Beth Eber, echoing the academy of Ever where Jacob studied.", :sources ["Genesis 25:27" "Rashi on Genesis 25:27 citing Megillah 17a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מבאר שבע", :translit "mi'be'er sheva", :gloss "from Be'er Sheba"} {:he "שם שגם נטמן בבית שם", :translit "shem she'gam nitman be'beit shem", :gloss "Shem, for he too was hidden in the house of Shem"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "מבאר שבע", :spells "שם"}], :frame {:text "The rashei tevot (initial letters) of 'מבאר שבע' spell שם (Shem). The reference to Shem being hidden in the house of Shem recalls the tradition that Jacob studied in the academy of Shem son of Noah before departing for Charan.", :sources ["Genesis 25:27" "Rashi on Genesis 25:27 citing Megillah 17a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "וילך", :translit "vayelech", :gloss "and he walked"} {:he "בו ביום", :translit "bo ba'yom", :gloss "on that very day"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "וילך", :b "בו ביום"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'בו ביום' ('on that very day') is a recurring expression in the Torah used to emphasize immediacy, as in Genesis 17:23 and 17:26 regarding Abraham's circumcision, and Exodus 12:17 and 12:51 regarding the Exodus. The gematria linking 'וילך' to 'בו ביום' suggests that Jacob's journey began that very same day he received his father's blessing.", :sources ["Genesis 17:23" "Genesis 17:26" "Exodus 12:17" "Exodus 12:51"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Parashah setumah observation: eleven instances in the Torah", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "ויצא יעקב מבאר", :a-value 532, :b "פנה זיוה הודה והדרה", :b-value 403, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "יעקב מבאר שבע", :spells "עבר", :letters ["ב" "ר" "ע"], :by :selah, :order :bag} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "ויצא יעקב מבאר שבע", :spells "ארבע", :letters ["א" "ב" "ר" "ע"], :by :selah, :order :bag} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "מבאר שבע", :spells "ער", :letters ["ר" "ע"], :by :selah, :order :bag} {:kind :other, :note "Letter play: shin of Sheva counts as ten (via at-bash or similar); combined with ער yields additional encoded readings", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "מבאר שבע", :spells "שם", :letters ["מ" "ש"], :by :selah, :order :bag} {:kind :gematria, :a "וילך", :a-value 66, :b "בו ביום", :b-value 66, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapte